---
[
  {
    "type": "function",
    "name": "read",
    "parameters": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "file_path": {
          "description": "Path of the file to read.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "range": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "additionalProperties": false,
              "properties": {
                "end_byte": {
                  "anyOf": [
                    {
                      "minimum": 0,
                      "type": "integer"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "null"
                    }
                  ],
                  "description": "0-based offset one past the last byte to read (byte range)."
                },
                "end_line": {
                  "anyOf": [
                    {
                      "type": "integer"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "null"
                    }
                  ],
                  "description": "Inclusive 1-based last line."
                },
                "start_byte": {
                  "anyOf": [
                    {
                      "minimum": 0,
                      "type": "integer"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "null"
                    }
                  ],
                  "description": "0-based byte offset of the first byte to read (byte range)."
                },
                "start_line": {
                  "anyOf": [
                    {
                      "type": "integer"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "null"
                    }
                  ],
                  "description": "1-based first line."
                }
              },
              "required": [
                "end_byte",
                "end_line",
                "start_byte",
                "start_line"
              ],
              "type": "object"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Optional line range (`start_line`/`end_line`) or byte range\n(`start_byte`/`end_byte`) for partial reads."
        },
        "show_line_numbers": {
          "default": true,
          "description": "If true, prefixes each line with its line number. Defaults to true.",
          "type": "boolean"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "file_path",
        "range",
        "show_line_numbers"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "strict": true,
    "description": "Read one file: text, PDF, image, or notebook. Relative paths resolve against the working directory.\n- Text comes back as `12:content`: line number, a colon, then the text. Lines are returned whole, however long they are. The prefix is not file content; strip it when you quote text back into an edit. `show_line_numbers: false` returns the text without prefixes.\n- A read stops after {{config.maxReadSize}} lines and ends with a footer naming the exact `range` that continues it. Use `range` with `start_line`/`end_line` for a slice of the file.\n- A whole-file read (no `range`) of a file larger than {{config.maxReadBytes}} bytes is refused with a suggested ranged call; ranged reads are not subject to that cap.\n- `range` also takes a byte range, `start_byte` (0-based) and optional `end_byte` (one past the last byte), for text with few or no line breaks: it returns raw text without line prefixes, one page at a time, snapped to UTF-8 character boundaries, and names the offset to continue from. Use it to page through a saved result file.\n- Pass either a line range or a byte range, never both.\n- Reading the same lines of an unchanged file again, while the earlier result is still in the conversation, returns a short note naming the read call that showed them.\n- A Jupyter notebook (.ipynb) read without a range comes back as its cells, each with its id, type, source and a summary of its outputs; a line or byte range reads its raw JSON.\n- A PDF comes back as extracted text (via `pdftotext`).\n{{#if (contains model.input_modalities \"image\")}}\n- Images (PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP) come back as images; a PDF with no extractable text comes back as an image. Images and image-fallback PDFs larger than {{config.maxImageSize}} bytes are refused.\n{{/if}}\n- Directories, pipes and other special files are refused; use `{{tool_names.fs_search}}` to find files.\n- Reading records which lines you have seen: `{{tool_names.patch}}` and an overwriting `{{tool_names.write}}` require a prior read of the file, and `{{tool_names.patch}}` requires that every line it changes was shown.\n- You can call multiple tools in a single response; read several files in parallel when you need them."
  },
  {
    "type": "function",
    "name": "write",
    "parameters": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "content": {
          "description": "The complete file content to write.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "file_path": {
          "description": "Path of the file to create or replace.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "overwrite": {
          "description": "Required to replace an existing file; the file must have been read\nfirst and must not have changed since.",
          "type": "boolean"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "content",
        "file_path",
        "overwrite"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "strict": true,
    "description": "Create a file, or replace one whole. Missing parent directories are created.\n- Replacing an existing file needs `overwrite=true` and a prior `{{tool_names.read}}` of the file in this conversation; one page is enough, the whole file is not needed. Without `overwrite=true` an existing file is refused.\n- If the file changed on disk since that read, the overwrite is refused and nothing is written; the error names both hashes and shows the diff from what you read to what is there now, and a retry after it goes through.\n- The file keeps its existing line-ending style (LF, CRLF or CR); a new file gets LF.\n- The previous state is snapshotted first, so `{{tool_names.undo}}` brings it back (undoing a create deletes the file).\n- After the write the file is parsed locally and syntax errors the write introduced are reported as a warning.\n- Edit existing files with `{{tool_names.patch}}`; use this only for new files or a deliberate full rewrite. Do not create documentation files unless asked."
  },
  {
    "type": "function",
    "name": "fs_search",
    "parameters": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "case_insensitive": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "boolean"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Match without case sensitivity."
        },
        "context": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Lines of context before and after each match (content mode)."
        },
        "context_after": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Lines of context after each match (content mode)."
        },
        "context_before": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Lines of context before each match (content mode)."
        },
        "file_type": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "File type, e.g. rust, py, js (ripgrep type names)."
        },
        "glob": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Glob filter on paths (e.g. \"*.rs\", \"*.{ts,tsx}\", \"!*.min.js\")."
        },
        "head_limit": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "minimum": 1,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Maximum result entries after `offset`."
        },
        "line_numbers": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "boolean"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Prefix content lines with their line numbers (default true)."
        },
        "multiline": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "boolean"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Let the regex span lines (`.` matches newlines)."
        },
        "offset": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "minimum": 0,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Result entries to skip before `head_limit`."
        },
        "output_mode": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "enum": [
                "content",
                "files_with_matches",
                "count"
              ],
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "\"content\" (matching lines), \"files_with_matches\" (paths, default) or\n\"count\" (matches per file)."
        },
        "path": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "File or directory to search (default: the current directory)."
        },
        "pattern": {
          "description": "Regex to search file contents for.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "case_insensitive",
        "context",
        "context_after",
        "context_before",
        "file_type",
        "glob",
        "head_limit",
        "line_numbers",
        "multiline",
        "offset",
        "output_mode",
        "path",
        "pattern"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "strict": true,
    "description": "Search file contents by regex. `output_mode` is `files_with_matches` (paths, the default), `content` (matching lines as `path:line:text`, context lines as `path-line-text`, `--` between groups) or `count` (`path:N`).\n- The search uses ripgrep's libraries in-process. Paths ignored by `.gitignore`/`.ignore` are skipped, as are hidden paths unless `path` names one or `glob` names a dot-leading component such as `.github/**`; binary files are skipped.\n- `glob` filters paths (a leading `!` excludes); `file_type` takes ripgrep type names (`rust`, `py`, `js`, …) or a bare extension, and cannot be combined with a positive `glob`.\n- Regex syntax is ripgrep's: escape literal braces (`interface\\{\\}`); `multiline: true` lets a pattern span lines.\n- Results are absolute paths in path order. Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 are shown as `\\xNN`.\n- The walk stays on the filesystem `path` is on and does not enter mount points below it; name a mounted share as `path` to search it. A search is bounded at 120 seconds.\n- `head_limit` and `offset` page through a large result; the result names the next `offset` when there are more. A result too large to show inline is written to a file whose path the result names.\n- Use `{{tool_names.read}}` to see a file once a search has found it, and `{{tool_names.task}}` for open-ended investigations that need several rounds of search and reading."
  },
  {
    "type": "function",
    "name": "ast_search",
    "parameters": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "description": "Input for the `ast_search` tool: structural search and rewrite.",
      "properties": {
        "apply": {
          "description": "Write the rewrite (default false).",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "language": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Language (e.g. rust, python, typescript, tsx, go, c, cpp); inferred\nfrom each file's extension when omitted."
        },
        "max_results": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "minimum": 1,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Most matches reported (default 200); an apply with more matches is\nrefused."
        },
        "path": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "File or directory to search (default: the current directory)."
        },
        "pattern": {
          "description": "ast-grep pattern; `$NAME` matches one node, `$$$NAME` zero or more.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "rewrite": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Replacement template using the pattern's metavariables; without\n`apply` the result is a diff and nothing is written."
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "apply",
        "language",
        "max_results",
        "path",
        "pattern",
        "rewrite"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "strict": true,
    "description": "Search code structurally with ast-grep (in-process, tree-sitter grammars). Patterns match parsed syntax, so they span whitespace and line breaks and never match inside comments or strings. Use `{{tool_names.fs_search}}` for plain text, regex and filenames.\n- In a pattern `$NAME` matches one syntax node and `$$$NAME` matches zero or more; metavariables are uppercase, and reusing one requires equal captures.\n- The language is inferred from each file's extension; set `language` to restrict the search to one language. Under `language=\"c\"` a bare call pattern like `call($A)` matches nothing, because tree-sitter-c parses that fragment as a type; write it as an expression statement (`call($A);`).\n- Matches come back as an absolute `path:line` heading, the numbered source lines (`12|code`), and the metavariable captures. When more matches exist than `max_results` (default 200), the extra ones are dropped and the result says so.\n- The walk skips paths ignored by `.gitignore` and hidden paths.\n- Rewrites are two-step: `rewrite` alone is a dry run that shows a unified diff and changes nothing; `apply=true` writes it and is refused when there are more matches than `max_results`. An applied rewrite snapshots every changed file for `{{tool_names.undo}}`."
  },
  {
    "type": "function",
    "name": "remove",
    "parameters": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "path": {
          "description": "Path of the file, directory or symlink to remove.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "permanent": {
          "description": "Delete directly instead of moving to the trash (default false).",
          "type": "boolean"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "path",
        "permanent"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "strict": true,
    "description": "Move a file, directory or symlink to the trash, or delete it with `permanent=true`. Either way the path is snapshotted first, so `{{tool_names.undo}}` can bring it back. Symlinks are removed as symlinks, never followed. The trash uses `trash` or `trash-put`; when neither is installed or the command fails, nothing is removed and `permanent=true` is the way to remove the path. Anything over 512 MiB is refused rather than trashed; confirm with the user before deleting that with `permanent=true`."
  },
  {
    "type": "function",
    "name": "patch",
    "parameters": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "description": "Input for the `patch` tool: either the scalar form (`old_string`,\n`new_string`, `replace_all`) or the batch form (`edits`), never both.",
      "properties": {
        "edits": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "items": {
                "additionalProperties": false,
                "description": "A single replacement inside a batch `patch` call.",
                "properties": {
                  "new_string": {
                    "description": "Replacement text; must differ from old_string.",
                    "type": "string"
                  },
                  "old_string": {
                    "description": "Exact text to replace.",
                    "type": "string"
                  },
                  "replace_all": {
                    "default": false,
                    "description": "Replace every occurrence of old_string instead of requiring exactly\none (default false).",
                    "type": "boolean"
                  }
                },
                "required": [
                  "new_string",
                  "old_string",
                  "replace_all"
                ],
                "type": "object"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Several replacements applied in order, each seeing the previous\nresult. Never combined with the scalar form."
        },
        "file_path": {
          "description": "Path of the file to modify.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "new_string": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Replacement text; must differ from old_string (scalar form)."
        },
        "old_string": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Exact text to replace (scalar form)."
        },
        "replace_all": {
          "description": "Replace every occurrence of old_string instead of requiring exactly\none (scalar form, default false).",
          "type": "boolean"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "edits",
        "file_path",
        "new_string",
        "old_string",
        "replace_all"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "strict": true,
    "description": "Replace exact text in one file.\n- Read the file with `{{tool_names.read}}` before editing it; editing a file this conversation has not read is refused. The `12:` prefix in read output is not file content; only the text after it is.\n- `old_string` must match exactly once unless `replace_all` is set. When it is not in the file exactly, it is matched again with smart quotes, Unicode dashes, no-break spaces and trailing whitespace read as plain ASCII; the file keeps its own characters wherever your edit left `old_string` unchanged. With `replace_all`, this fallback runs only when there is no exact occurrence. A miss reports the closest line in the file.\n- Pass `edits` (a list of `{old_string, new_string, replace_all}`) for several replacements in one call: they apply in order, each seeing the previous result, and all are validated before anything is written, so a failed call leaves the file untouched and names the offending edit. Never combine `edits` with the scalar `old_string`/`new_string`/`replace_all` form.\n- Line endings in `old_string`/`new_string` are matched and written in the file's own style (LF or CRLF).\n- After the write, the file is parsed locally and any syntax errors the edit introduced are reported as a warning.\n- One call is one `{{tool_names.undo}}` step.\n- Use `{{tool_names.write}}` for new files or a deliberate full rewrite."
  },
  {
    "type": "function",
    "name": "undo",
    "parameters": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "path": {
          "description": "Path whose latest snapshot should be restored.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "path"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "strict": true,
    "description": "Restore one path to its latest snapshot. Every tool that changes a file (`{{tool_names.write}}`, `{{tool_names.patch}}`, `{{tool_names.remove}}`) snapshots it first; one `{{tool_names.patch}}` call, even with several edits, is one snapshot.\n- Restores file contents and permission modes, symlink targets, whole directory trees, and absence: undoing the creation of a file deletes it. Ownership, timestamps and extended attributes are not restored.\n- Each successful restore pops one snapshot, so repeated calls walk further back. A filesystem error keeps the snapshot for a retry. An unusable snapshot is discarded with an error without changing the path; call again to reach an older entry.\n- Snapshots belong to this conversation and survive a restart of it; the store keeps the newest 100 entries (64 MiB). This is not git and does not touch repository history."
  },
  {
    "type": "function",
    "name": "shell",
    "parameters": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "action": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "description": "Start `command` and wait up to `timeout` seconds for it.",
              "enum": [
                "run"
              ],
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "description": "Report a running job's new output without waiting.",
              "enum": [
                "poll"
              ],
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "description": "Wait up to `timeout` more seconds for a running job.",
              "enum": [
                "wait"
              ],
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "description": "Stop a running job (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL, to its process group).",
              "enum": [
                "kill"
              ],
              "type": "string"
            }
          ],
          "description": "run (default), poll, wait or kill."
        },
        "command": {
          "default": "",
          "description": "The command to run (required for action \"run\").",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "cwd": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Working directory for the command (default: the current directory)."
        },
        "description": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Short description of what the command does."
        },
        "env": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "items": {
                "type": "string"
              },
              "type": "array"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Extra environment variable names to pass through, on top of the\nconfigured allow-list."
        },
        "handle": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "type": "string"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Job id from a `HANDLE n RUNNING` line, for poll/wait/kill (default:\nthe most recent running job)."
        },
        "keep_ansi": {
          "description": "Keep ANSI escape codes in the output (default false).",
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "timeout": {
          "anyOf": [
            {
              "minimum": 1,
              "type": "integer"
            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ],
          "description": "Seconds to wait before returning a handle (run) or to keep waiting\n(wait); default 30. The command is never killed when the window ends."
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "action",
        "command",
        "cwd",
        "description",
        "env",
        "handle",
        "keep_ansi",
        "timeout"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "strict": true,
    "description": "Run one command with `{{env.shell}}` and return its exit code, stdout and stderr. Under bash and zsh a pipeline fails when any stage fails (`pipefail`), so `false | cat` reports exit 1. The working directory is `cwd`, default `{{env.cwd}}`; set `cwd` instead of using `cd`.\n\nLong output comes back as its head and its tail, with a marker between them naming the bytes left out and the file that holds the whole stream:\n\n    [truncated: output budget (… bytes) reached; stdout bytes 25000-980000 of 1004000 (955000 bytes) are not shown; the whole stdout is at /path/shell-3-….log — read it on with range {\"start_byte\": 25000}]\n\nRead any part of that file with `{{tool_names.read}}` and a byte range, so do not pipe through `head` or `tail` just to shrink the output. Stdin is `/dev/null` and there is no terminal: a command that reads input gets end-of-file, and a password or confirmation prompt gets no answer.\n\nA command does not have to finish before you get an answer. The call waits `timeout` seconds (default 30); a command that finishes inside that window comes back whole, and one still running comes back as a handle with whatever it has printed so far:\n\n    command still running after 30s (handle 3, pid 4242). ...\n    HANDLE 3 RUNNING\n\nThe command is never killed by the wait. Then `action=\"poll\", handle=\"3\"` returns new output and whether it is still running, `action=\"wait\", handle=\"3\", timeout=N` blocks up to N more seconds, and `action=\"kill\", handle=\"3\"` stops it (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL, to its whole process group). `handle` defaults to the most recent running job. Do a poll before assuming a long build or test run has failed. Interrupting the turn kills a command the call is still waiting on.\n\nThe command gets a filtered environment: the configured allow-list (by default PATH, HOME, USER, LANG, LC_ALL, TERM, PWD, SHELL) plus the names you list in `env`. When the command references a variable that was filtered out, the result says so.\n\n- Independent commands can go in parallel calls in one response; chain dependent ones with `&&` in one call.\n- Search with `{{tool_names.fs_search}}`, not `grep`, `rg`, or `find`.\n- Read files with `{{tool_names.read}}`, not `cat`, `head`, or `tail`.\n- Edit files with `{{tool_names.patch}}`, not `sed` or `awk`.\n- Write files with `{{tool_names.write}}`, not redirects or heredocs.\n- Delete with `{{tool_names.remove}}`, not `rm`."
  },
  {
    "type": "function",
    "name": "fetch",
    "parameters": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "description": "Input type for the net fetch tool",
      "properties": {
        "body": {
          "description": "Request body text.",
          "nullable": true,
          "type": "string"
        },
        "headers": {
          "additionalProperties": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "description": "Request headers, name to value.",
          "nullable": true,
          "type": "object"
        },
        "json_body": {
          "additionalProperties": true,
          "description": "Request body as JSON (sets Content-Type: application/json); not with\n`body`.",
          "nullable": true,
          "type": "object"
        },
        "method": {
          "description": "HTTP method (default GET).",
          "nullable": true,
          "type": "string"
        },
        "raw": {
          "description": "Return the body as is, without HTML-to-markdown conversion (default\nfalse).",
          "nullable": true,
          "type": "boolean"
        },
        "save": {
          "description": "Stream the response body to this path instead of returning it; no size\nlimit, resumable.",
          "nullable": true,
          "type": "string"
        },
        "url": {
          "description": "URL to fetch: http(s):// or file://.",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "url"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "strict": false,
    "description": "Fetch a URL over HTTP(S) or `file://`.\n\nHTML comes back as markdown with links made absolute, so you can fetch them as-is; `raw=true` returns the source instead. `method`, `headers`, `body` and `json_body` make any HTTP request (a GET that saves nothing checks robots.txt first). `save` streams the body to that path instead of returning it, with no size limit; a partial download left by an interrupted save resumes with a Range request, and the previous file at that path can be brought back with `{{tool_names.undo}}`. Binary responses come back as a short descriptor naming the type and size; pass `save` to get the bytes. Images come back as images when the model accepts image input. Unsaved responses are read up to 32 MiB and bounded by a 30-second timeout; a large text result is written to a file whose path and continuation range the result names."
  },
  {
    "type": "function",
    "name": "plan",
    "parameters": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "content": {
          "description": "The content to write to the plan file. This should be the complete\nplan content in markdown format.",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "plan_name": {
          "description": "The name of the plan (will be used in the filename)",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "version": {
          "description": "The version of the plan (e.g., \"v1\", \"v2\", \"1.0\")",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "content",
        "plan_name",
        "version"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "strict": true,
    "description": "Creates a new plan file with the specified name, version, and content. Use this tool to create structured project plans, task breakdowns, or implementation strategies that can be tracked and referenced throughout development sessions."
  },
  {
    "type": "function",
    "name": "skill",
